                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "past_continuous",
                    "title": "Past continuous vs past simple",
                    "explanation": "El past continuous («was» / «were» + «-ing») describe una acción que estaba en curso en un momento del pasado:\n- «At nine I was sitting on the bus.»\n\nLo combinamos con el past simple para contar que algo interrumpió esa acción. La acción larga va en continuous y la corta, en simple:\n- «I was walking to the bus stop when it started to rain.»\n\nCon «while» solemos usar el continuous:\n- «While I was opening it, the wind broke it.»\n\nPregunta típica:\n- «What were you doing when Helen called?»",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Sujeto",
                        "Afirmativa",
                        "Negativa",
                        "Pregunta"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "I / he / she / it",
                          "was walking",
                          "wasn't walking",
                          "Was she walking?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "you / we / they",
                          "were walking",
                          "weren't walking",
                          "Were they walking?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I was walking to the bus stop when it started to rain.",
                      "While I was opening it, the wind broke it.",
                      "What were you doing when Helen called?",
                      "She wasn't angry. She was talking to a client."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "sequencing_adverbs",
                    "title": "Telling a story: first, then, finally · adverbs of manner",
                    "explanation": "Para ordenar lo que pasó usamos conectores de secuencia: «First» (primero), «Then» / «After that» (luego, después), «Later» (más tarde), «Finally» / «In the end» (al final). Van al principio de la frase:\n- «First, it started to rain. Then the wind broke my umbrella.»\n\nLos adverbios de modo dicen cómo pasa algo. Se forman con adjetivo + «-ly»: «quick» → «quickly», «slow» → «slowly», «careful» → «carefully». Si acaba en y, cambia a «-ily»: «happy» → «happily», «angry» → «angrily».\n\nIrregulares: «good» → «well», «fast» → «fast», «hard» → «hard», «late» → «late».\n\nVan después del verbo o del objeto:\n- «She ran quickly.»\n- «He drives his car carefully.»\n\nOjo: «She speaks English well» (nunca «good»).",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Adjetivo",
                        "Adverbio",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "quick",
                          "quickly",
                          "I walked quickly to the bus stop."
                        ],
                        [
                          "careful",
                          "carefully",
                          "Carry the coffee carefully!"
                        ],
                        [
                          "happy",
                          "happily",
                          "The children played happily."
                        ],
                        [
                          "good",
                          "well",
                          "Helen speaks Spanish very well."
                        ],
                        [
                          "fast / hard",
                          "fast / hard",
                          "It was raining hard."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "First, it started to rain. Then the wind broke my umbrella.",
                      "After that, a man ran into me on the bus.",
                      "Finally, I arrived at the office, wet and late.",
                      "I walked quickly, but it was raining very hard."
                    ]
                  }
                },
